TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "Player roles based on offensive play types (cuts vs. drives)",
    "Role generalizability and transferability across team contexts",
    "Player impact metrics"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to categorize players as 'cut-heavy' or 'drive-heavy' shapers?",
    "How to define and measure 'role travel' across different team contexts?",
    "What metrics best quantify player impact for comparison?",
    "How to control for confounding factors like team quality, coaching, and player development/aging?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish anything about this research question, its concepts, or suitable methodologies."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The derived play-type possession records are suitable for categorizing players by their offensive play-type usage (cuts, drives).",
    "Multiple seasons of data allow for observation of players changing teams, which can serve as a proxy for context change.",
    "Quantifying 'role travel' will require defining a player impact metric and comparing its stability or change across different contexts."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition of 'shaper' is not provided and needs to be operationalized.",
    "The precise definition of 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' (e.g., thresholds, ratios) is not specified.",
    "The meaning of 'n=24 units' is ambiguous. If this refers to players who change teams or contexts, it is a very small sample size.",
    "How to quantify 'travel better' needs to be defined (e.g., stability of an impact metric, relative performance change)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available, so it did not change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.2,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "The stated 'n=24 units' is extremely small for a study requiring player categorization into two groups ('cut-heavy', 'drive-heavy') and observing their performance across different team contexts (i.e., changing teams). This severely limits statistical power for detecting any meaningful effect and makes it highly susceptible to noise and outliers."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "It is uncertain if there will be a sufficient number of players who clearly fit the 'cut-heavy shaper' or 'drive-heavy shaper' archetypes AND who also change teams during the available seasons to form an adequate sample for analysis, exacerbating the sample size issue."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work was retrieved that directly addresses this specific research question or related methodological challenges, leaving it unanswered."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The 'n=24 units' is critically low. This will make it difficult to achieve statistical significance, properly categorize players, and draw robust conclusions about role transferability."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "There may not be enough players who distinctly fit the 'cut-heavy shaper' or 'drive-heavy shaper' definitions and also experience a meaningful context change (e.g., changing teams) within the available data window."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Player performance upon changing teams can be confounded by numerous factors: the quality of the new team/teammates, coaching philosophy, changes in player age/development/injury status, and the specific reasons for the team change (e.g., a player underperforming or seeking a different role)."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Players who change teams are not a random sample. Their movement might be driven by prior performance (good or bad), which could introduce regression to the mean effects or selection bias, making it difficult to isolate the effect of role transferability."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The reliability of player impact metrics (e.g., adjusted plus-minus, advanced on-off metrics) might be low for individual player-season observations, especially for players with lower minute totals or less stable roles, impacting the ability to accurately assess 'role travel'."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.6,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The difference in the stability of player impact (e.g., Net Rating, adjusted offensive efficiency) when changing teams, comparing 'cut-heavy shapers' to 'drive-heavy shapers'. Stability is defined as the absolute change or percentage change in impact metric from the player's previous team to their new team.",
    "unit": "Player-team-change instance. Each instance represents a player's performance on a new team compared to their immediate prior team.",
    "sample": "All players identified as 'shapers' (e.g., top quartile in offensive possessions initiated per 100 possessions) who change teams between the 2022-23 and 2025-26 seasons. Players are then categorized into 'cut-heavy' (e.g., top 25th percentile in cut-to-drive possession ratio among shapers) and 'drive-heavy' (e.g., bottom 25th percentile in cut-to-drive possession ratio among shapers). Minimum 500 possessions initiated per season for inclusion.",
    "power": "Not estimable with n=24 units. A robust study would likely require hundreds of player-team-change instances across archetypes to achieve sufficient power for detecting a meaningful effect.",
    "controls": "Control for player age, prior team quality (e.g., win percentage), new team quality, and change in player usage rate. This would ideally be done via a regression framework (e.g., difference-in-differences, fixed effects model if multiple team changes per player).",
    "falsification": "The null hypothesis is that there is no significant difference in the stability of impact between cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers when changing teams. Falsification would involve demonstrating a statistically significant difference in stability.",
    "multiplicity": "If multiple impact metrics are used, adjust p-values for multiple comparisons (e.g., Bonferroni correction or False Discovery Rate).",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis if the p-value for the difference in stability between the two groups is less than 0.05, after accounting for controls and multiplicity.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular season and playoffs. Partial 2025-26 regular season and playoffs data would be used only for players who change teams into 2025-26, ensuring sufficient post-change data."
  }
}

